window.BOOK_FC=[{"t":"Sources of evidence and databases","s":12,"e":18,"c":[{"q":"Rank these for a treatment question: case-control study, RCT, expert opinion, systematic review of RCTs, cohort study.","a":"Systematic review of RCTs, then RCT, cohort, case-control, expert opinion. The review keeps randomisation's protection against bias and adds precision; opinion contributes no data of its own.","p":13},{"q":"A registry cohort of 182,400 versus a meta-analysis of 14 RCTs (3,218 participants): which carries more weight on whether fluoxetine works?","a":"The meta-analysis. Design beats size: in the cohort people chose treatment, so severity confounds. The registry is still the better source for rare harms.","p":14},{"q":"When can a systematic review at the top of the hierarchy mislead you?","a":"When it pools small, biased trials: you get a precise wrong answer. GRADE then rates certainty down for bias, inconsistency, indirectness, imprecision or publication bias.","p":14},{"q":"What is the best primary design for a screening-test accuracy question, and for a prognosis question?","a":"Accuracy: a cross-sectional comparison of the test against a reference standard. Prognosis: an inception cohort. The treatment ladder does not fit every question.","p":14},{"q":"Distinguish primary from secondary research, with examples.","a":"Primary research is the original study, such as a trial or cohort. Secondary research summarises primary studies: systematic reviews, meta-analyses, guidelines and economic analyses.","p":13},{"q":"Which database is best for nursing literature, for European drug trials and conference abstracts, and for psychology books and dissertations?","a":"CINAHL; Embase, indexed with Emtree; PsycInfo. MEDLINE uses MeSH headings, and the Cochrane Library holds reviews (CDSR) and the CENTRAL trials register.","p":12},{"q":"In a Venn diagram, clozapine and (neutropenia OR agranulocytosis) overlap in 612 records outside an adolescent circle and 47 inside it. How many does the AND search return?","a":"659. AND keeps every record in both circles, including the centre where all three meet. Forgetting the centre gives 612.","p":15,"f":"Records = 612 + 47 = 659"},{"q":"You want schizophrenia, psychosis and psychotic disorder treated as one concept, then combined with clozapine. Which operators go where?","a":"OR inside the concept, AND between concepts. OR widens the net and raises sensitivity; AND keeps only records with every concept. NOT is risky because it can throw out relevant records.","p":17},{"q":"A strategy retrieves 2,900 records containing 62 of 64 known relevant trials. Calculate its sensitivity and precision.","a":"Sensitivity 97%, precision 2.1%. For a systematic review this is right: screening many records is the price of not missing trials, which would bias the review.","p":16,"f":"Sensitivity = 62 / 64 = 97% · Precision = 62 / 2,900 = 2.1%"},{"q":"What do truncation, phrase searching and proximity operators each do?","a":"Truncation (anorexi*) finds every word ending. Quotation marks force an exact phrase such as \"self harm\". Proximity operators find words within a set distance, in any order.","p":17}]},{"t":"Framing clinical questions: PICO","s":19,"e":24,"c":[{"q":"Frame as PICO(T): in first-episode psychosis, does family intervention reduce relapse within 12 months compared with usual care? Which design answers it?","a":"P first-episode psychosis; I family intervention; C usual care; O relapse; T 12 months. Intervention versus comparison on an outcome is a therapy question, so a randomised controlled trial.","p":20},{"q":"When should you write PECO(T) rather than PICO(T)?","a":"When the factor is an exposure that cannot be assigned, such as cannabis or migration, in questions of aetiology, harm or prognosis. E replaces I, and an observational design follows.","p":19},{"q":"How do PICO blocks become a database search, and which elements usually stay out of it?","a":"Synonyms within a block are joined with OR; blocks are joined with AND. The comparison is often omitted, as abstracts rarely name it, and the time frame guides screening.","p":21},{"q":"What separates a background question from a foreground question?","a":"Background asks general knowledge, such as what family intervention is, and a textbook answers it. Foreground asks about a specific decision for a specific patient and needs research evidence.","p":20},{"q":"Match each question type to its best design: therapy, diagnosis, prognosis, frequency, patient experience.","a":"Therapy: RCT. Diagnosis: cross-sectional, everyone gets test and reference standard. Prognosis: inception cohort. Frequency: survey. Experience: qualitative study.","p":20},{"q":"A trainee proposes a prospective cohort comparing families who chose family work with those who did not. What is the flaw?","a":"Confounding: families who opt in differ in motivation and support, which also affect relapse. Being prospective does not fix this; if the intervention can be randomised, use an RCT.","p":21},{"q":"A neonatal complication occurs at 0.1 per 1,000 person-years. How many people must a cohort follow for 5 years to see 100 cases?","a":"200,000. A case-control study with 100 cases and 400 controls needs only 500 people, so for a rare outcome start from the cases.","p":22,"f":"N = 100 / (0.1 / 1,000 × 5) = 200,000"},{"q":"Which design does a rare outcome favour, and which does a rare exposure favour?","a":"Rare outcome: case-control, starting from the few cases and looking back. Rare exposure: cohort, recruiting the few exposed and following them forward.","p":21},{"q":"Why is a case-control design a poor way to study diagnostic accuracy?","a":"Comparing clear-cut cases with healthy controls drops the hard middle of the spectrum, so accuracy looks better than in practice (spectrum bias). Use a cross-sectional sample of real patients.","p":21},{"q":"Pharmacists are trained in brief alcohol advice, and customers of trained and untrained pharmacies may talk. Which design?","a":"A cluster randomised trial: randomise whole pharmacies, since the intervention is delivered at that level and individual randomisation would allow contamination.","p":23}]},{"t":"Cause and effect: Bradford Hill","s":25,"e":29,"c":[{"q":"Before calling an association causal, which four alternative explanations must you rule out?","a":"Chance, bias, confounding and reverse causation. Only once these are dealt with do frameworks such as Bradford Hill help you judge causation.","p":25},{"q":"Which of the nine Bradford Hill viewpoints is essential?","a":"Temporality alone: the exposure must come before the outcome. The others (strength, consistency, specificity, gradient, plausibility, coherence, experiment, analogy) add weight but are not a checklist to be passed.","p":26},{"q":"Odds of psychosis rise from 1.2 for less-than-weekly cannabis to 4.8 for daily high-potency use. Which viewpoint is this, and does it prove causation?","a":"Biological gradient (dose-response). It strengthens the case, as a confounder would have to rise in step, but reverse causation from prodromal symptoms and confounding by childhood adversity still need excluding.","p":26},{"q":"A cohort drinking only 0 to 30 units a week shows no gradient in cognitive impairment, yet risk truly rises above 35 units. Explain.","a":"Everyone sat below the threshold, on the flat part of the curve. Missing dose-response does not exclude causation: thresholds, narrow exposure ranges, confounding or chance can hide it.","p":28},{"q":"Childhood adversity is linked to psychosis in studies from Sweden, New Zealand, the UK and the Netherlands, using different designs. Which viewpoint?","a":"Consistency. The same association across places, populations and designs makes a shared artefact unlikely.","p":29},{"q":"Recent psychiatric discharge carries a very large relative risk of suicide. Which viewpoint does this support, and why does it help?","a":"Strength of association. A huge effect is hard to explain away by an unmeasured confounder, though not impossible.","p":29},{"q":"Cannabis is linked to several outcomes, not just psychosis. Which Bradford Hill viewpoint does it fail, and does that matter much?","a":"Specificity, meaning one exposure leads to one outcome. Lacking it does not sink a causal case, because only temporality is required.","p":26},{"q":"In Rothman's causal pies, what are sufficient, component and necessary causes?","a":"A sufficient cause is a complete pie; each slice is a component cause, not enough alone; a necessary cause appears in every pie. Most outcomes need several components acting together.","p":27},{"q":"Genetic liability appears in two of three causal pies. Is it a necessary cause, and what does removing one slice achieve?","a":"No: necessary means present in every pie. Removing any slice prevents only the cases its pies would have caused, not every case.","p":27},{"q":"Why do Koch's postulates fit schizophrenia poorly, and what three things did Susser ask for?","a":"Koch built them for single infectious agents isolated from disease; psychiatric disorders are multifactorial. Susser asked for association, time order and direction.","p":25}]},{"t":"Incidence, prevalence, ratios and rates","s":30,"e":36,"c":[{"q":"Twelve people on a first-generation antipsychotic contribute 42.3 person-years; 4 develop tardive dyskinesia. What is the incidence rate?","a":"9.5 per 100 person-years. Each person contributes time until event, exit or study end. Dividing by 12 people gives 33%, ignoring unequal follow-up.","p":31,"f":"Incidence rate = 4 / 42.3 × 100 = 9.5 per 100 person-years"},{"q":"How does an incidence risk differ from an incidence rate?","a":"Risk is new cases over people at risk at the start, for a stated period, and needs full follow-up. Rate is new cases over person-time at risk, suiting open populations and unequal follow-up.","p":31},{"q":"In that cohort, two people are lost and one dies of another cause. How do they enter the incidence rate?","a":"They are censored: their observed person-time goes in the denominator but they add no events. Counting them as cases inflates the rate; lost is not ill.","p":32},{"q":"What is the difference between point and period prevalence, and when do you use prevalence rather than incidence?","a":"Point: existing cases at one moment over the population. Period: anyone affected during the period. Prevalence plans services; incidence studies causes.","p":31},{"q":"Incidence is 2 per 1,000 per year and illness lasts 20 years on average. Estimate steady-state prevalence.","a":"About 4% (the simulation settles at 3.85%). Same inflow, but cases stay 20 times longer, so the pool holds about 20 times more than a one-year illness.","p":33,"f":"P ≈ I × D = 0.002 × 20 = 0.04"},{"q":"A new drug cures depression faster but does not change who becomes ill. What happens to incidence and prevalence? And for a drug that prevents death without cure?","a":"Faster cure: prevalence falls, incidence unchanged, because duration shortens. Preventing death without cure: prevalence rises, incidence unchanged.","p":32},{"q":"Psychosis develops in 42 of 1,500 heavy cannabis users and 68 of 8,500 non-users. Calculate the attributable risk and number needed to harm.","a":"AR 2.0%; NNH 50. Assuming causation, about 20 in every 1,000 heavy users develop psychosis because of their use.","p":34,"f":"AR = 42 / 1,500 − 68 / 8,500 = 2.8% − 0.8% = 2.0% · NNH = 1 / 0.02 = 50"},{"q":"The relative risk of psychosis for heavy cannabis users is 3.5. What proportion of their psychosis is due to heavy use?","a":"71%: the attributable fraction in the exposed, assuming causation. The rest would have happened anyway.","p":35,"f":"AF(exposed) = (RR − 1) / RR = 2.5 / 3.5 = 71%"},{"q":"Psychosis risk is 1.10% in the whole cohort and 0.80% in the unexposed. Give the population attributable risk and fraction.","a":"PAR 0.30%; PAF 27%. About a quarter of the cohort's psychosis would vanish without heavy use, assuming causation.","p":35,"f":"PAR = 1.10% − 0.80% = 0.30% · PAF = 0.30 / 1.10 = 27%"},{"q":"Define endemic, epidemic, outbreak and pandemic.","a":"Endemic: the usual level. Epidemic: clearly above expected for that place and time. Outbreak: the same, usually smaller and local. Pandemic: an epidemic across countries or continents. Judge against expectation, not a fixed count.","p":32}]},{"t":"Mortality statistics and standardisation","s":37,"e":43,"c":[{"q":"Trust South's crude death rate is 41.9 per 1,000, North's 18.6, yet South's rate is lower in every age band. How can both be true?","a":"South's register is older. A crude rate is an average of age-specific rates weighted by age structure, so more people in high-risk old bands inflate it. Age confounds the crude comparison.","p":38},{"q":"After direct standardisation the North scores 22.3 and the South 20.0 per 1,000. What has happened to the crude ordering, and why trust the standardised figures?","a":"It reverses. Both trusts' age-specific rates are now applied to one shared standard population, so age structure no longer differs and the comparison is like for like.","p":38,"f":"Direct rate = Σ(study rate in band × standard pop in band) / standard total"},{"q":"A register has 293 observed deaths; applying general-population age-specific rates to its age structure predicts 156.3. Calculate and interpret the SMR.","a":"SMR ≈ 187. Deaths ran at almost twice the number expected had this group died at general-population rates for its age mix.","p":38,"f":"SMR = O / E × 100 = 293 / 156.3 × 100 ≈ 187"},{"q":"A forensic unit has 85 patients and four deaths in five years. Should mortality be standardised directly or indirectly, and what does it yield?","a":"Indirect, giving an SMR. Four deaths cannot give stable age-specific rates for direct standardisation; indirect borrows stable rates from a large standard and needs only the unit's age structure and total deaths.","p":40},{"q":"A cohort's cancer SMR is 107 (95% CI 92 to 123). Is cancer mortality raised compared with the general population?","a":"Not shown. The interval includes 100, so the result is compatible with deaths exactly as expected. A point estimate above 100 is not enough on its own.","p":41},{"q":"In a schizophrenia cohort, suicide has SMR 1204 (118 observed, 9.8 expected) and cardiovascular disease SMR 210 (362 observed, 172 expected). Which causes more excess deaths?","a":"Cardiovascular disease: about 190 excess deaths against about 108 for suicide. A huge SMR on a small expected count can mean fewer extra deaths than a modest SMR on a common cause.","p":41,"f":"Excess deaths = O − E"},{"q":"Two trusts each report an indirectly standardised SMR, 140 and 120. Why is it a trap to conclude the first performs worse?","a":"Each SMR is standardised to its own population's age structure, so SMRs are not directly comparable with each other. To rank populations, use direct standardisation onto one common standard.","p":40},{"q":"A colleague reads an SMR of 100 as 'no deaths occurred'. Correct them.","a":"An SMR of 100 means observed deaths equalled expected deaths: mortality exactly as in the standard population. Above 100 means excess deaths; below 100 fewer than expected.","p":40},{"q":"A woman dies by suicide five months after delivery from postpartum psychosis. How is her death classified, and what is the maternal mortality ratio's denominator?","a":"A late maternal death (42 days to one year after pregnancy ends). The maternal mortality ratio counts deaths within 42 days per 100,000 live births; the maternal mortality rate uses women of reproductive age instead.","p":42},{"q":"A commissioner uses an area's high Index of Multiple Deprivation rank to label every resident deprived. What is wrong with that?","a":"The IMD ranks small areas on seven weighted domains; it describes places, not people. Not everyone in a deprived area is deprived, so applying it to individuals misleads.","p":39}]},{"t":"Bias: selection, information","s":44,"e":49,"c":[{"q":"An investigator plans to triple the sample size to eliminate bias from a study. Will this work?","a":"No. Bias is systematic error; a larger sample shrinks only random error, giving a narrower confidence interval around the same wrong answer. Bias must be prevented by design.","p":44},{"q":"In the community, 6% of young people with and without ADHD self-harm. Among inpatients, 86% versus 62%. What bias explains the inpatient association?","a":"Berkson's (admission rate) bias. Having both problems makes admission more likely, so the ward over-represents people with both and manufactures an association. Draw samples from the community instead.","p":45},{"q":"Inpatient data: ADHD with self-harm 120, ADHD without 19, no ADHD with self-harm 288, no ADHD without 180. Calculate the odds ratio.","a":"OR ≈ 3.9. This ward-based figure is spurious; the community odds ratio was 1.0, so the hospital route into the sample created the association.","p":45,"f":"OR = ad / bc = (120 × 180) / (19 × 288) ≈ 3.9"},{"q":"A PTSD case-control study recruits long-standing clinic attenders, missing people who recovered quickly or died early. Name the bias and its remedy.","a":"Neyman (prevalence-incidence) bias. Prevalent cases are selected survivors, missing brief or fatal cases. Recruit incident (newly diagnosed) cases instead.","p":45},{"q":"Screen-detected dementia patients survive 8 years from diagnosis versus 5 years for symptomatic diagnosis, but die at the same age. Explain.","a":"Lead-time bias. Screening moved diagnosis 3 years earlier without delaying death, so survival from diagnosis lengthens artificially. Compare mortality in randomised screened versus unscreened groups.","p":47,"f":"Apparent gain = 8 − 5 = 3 years of lead time"},{"q":"Why might cases detected by a single screening round have better outcomes even if screening achieves nothing?","a":"Length-time bias. Slowly progressing disease has a longer detectable preclinical phase, so a one-off screen preferentially catches indolent cases, which do better anyway.","p":47},{"q":"A funnel plot of 17 omega-3 trials has an empty bottom-left corner; Egger's test p = 0.009. Interpret this.","a":"Small-study effects, most likely publication bias: small null or negative trials are missing. The pooled estimate is inflated (0.12 published versus 0.10 for all trials). Search registries and grey literature.","p":48},{"q":"A rater who knows which children have ADHD scores classroom videos for hyperactivity. What bias threatens this, and how is it prevented?","a":"Observer (diagnostic suspicion) bias: knowing group membership colours the rating. Blind the assessor to group status.","p":48},{"q":"Patients underreport drinking when interviewed face to face with their GP present. Name the bias and a fix.","a":"Social desirability bias: people give answers they expect to be viewed favourably. Use anonymous or self-completed questionnaires.","p":48},{"q":"A trial-planning colleague asks which safeguards counter selection, detection, attrition and reporting bias. Match them.","a":"Selection: allocation concealment. Performance and detection: blinding. Attrition: intention-to-treat analysis. Reporting: trial registration. A ward scale reading 2 kg heavy is instrument bias; calibrate it.","p":44}]},{"t":"Confounding and DAGs","s":50,"e":55,"c":[{"q":"Crude risk ratio for dementia with long-term benzodiazepines is 1.55; within both age bands and Mantel-Haenszel adjusted it is 1.10. What role does age play?","a":"Confounder. Users are older (70% aged 75+ versus 40% of non-users) and age causes dementia. Adjusting shrinks the estimate, while matching stratum estimates show no effect modification.","p":51},{"q":"Calculate the percentage change from the crude risk ratio of 1.55 to the adjusted 1.10, and state what it implies.","a":"About 29%, well beyond the 10% rule of thumb, so age is an important confounder.","p":52,"f":"Change = (1.55 − 1.10) / 1.55 × 100 ≈ 29%"},{"q":"State the three conditions for a variable to be a confounder.","a":"It is associated with the exposure, it is a cause of (or risk factor for) the outcome, and it does not lie on the causal path between them. Association with the outcome alone is not enough.","p":50},{"q":"A drug's risk ratio is 0.6 in men and 2.5 in women. Should you report one pooled adjusted estimate?","a":"No. Stratum-specific estimates that differ indicate effect modification, which is a finding in its own right. Report each stratum separately rather than averaging them away.","p":52},{"q":"In a DAG, antidepressant use in pregnancy points to preterm birth, which points to child ADHD. Should analysts adjust for preterm birth?","a":"No: preterm birth is a mediator, carrying part of the effect. Adjusting for it removes part of the real effect (over-adjustment).","p":53},{"q":"In the same DAG, both antidepressant exposure and child ADHD point into 'referred for paediatric follow-up'. What is it, and why not select on it?","a":"A collider. Conditioning on it, by adjustment or by sampling only referred children, opens a false path and creates a spurious association. Berkson's bias is a form of this.","p":53},{"q":"Service A has more remissions overall, but service B does better within every severity stratum. Name the phenomenon and the fair comparison.","a":"Simpson's paradox: extreme confounding reverses the association because the services treat very different case mixes. The stratified comparison shows which service does better for a given patient.","p":52},{"q":"Twelve clozapine patients have propensity scores above anyone in the comparison group. What does this mean for the analysis?","a":"They lie outside common support: there are no comparable controls, so they cannot be matched and any estimate for them rests on extrapolation.","p":54},{"q":"A researcher claims propensity score matching has balanced all confounders, measured and unmeasured. Is that right?","a":"No. Propensity scores balance only the measured covariates in the model. Only randomisation balances unknown confounders.","p":54},{"q":"A case-control study makes controls' overall age and sex mix match the cases without pairing individuals. Name the method and contrast it with minimisation.","a":"Frequency (group) matching, a design-stage control. Minimisation, used in small trials, allocates each new participant to whichever arm best keeps chosen factors balanced.","p":55}]},{"t":"Case reports and cross-sectional studies","s":56,"e":62,"c":[{"q":"Across 32 local authorities, antidepressant prescribing correlates with suicide rates (r = 0.80). A commentator says individuals on antidepressants are at higher risk. Name the error.","a":"Ecological fallacy. Each point is an area; we do not know whether those who died took antidepressants. Area deprivation could drive both prescribing and suicide.","p":57},{"q":"What is the atomistic fallacy, and how does it differ from the ecological fallacy?","a":"Atomistic: wrongly applying an individual-level association to groups. Ecological is the reverse: applying a group-level association to individuals.","p":57},{"q":"Between areas the alcohol-anxiety slope is −0.54 GAD-7 points per unit; within areas it is +0.37. What does this show?","a":"Area and individual associations can differ even in direction. Affluent areas may drink more yet be less anxious, while within each area heavier drinkers are more anxious. Area data alone would mislead.","p":59},{"q":"When is an ecological study the natural design rather than a weak substitute?","a":"When the exposure is a genuine group property, such as alcohol taxation or a change in the law. Ecological studies are also cheap and use routine data.","p":57},{"q":"Eleven inpatients develop hyponatraemia within a month of starting an SSRI and are described in a report. What design is this, and what can it not estimate?","a":"A case series. Without a comparison group or denominator it cannot estimate risk or frequency; it generates hypotheses only.","p":61},{"q":"A household survey finds 78 of 410 adults aged 16 to 24 have PHQ-9 ≥ 10. What measure is this, and what is its value?","a":"Prevalence, about 19%. A single cross-sectional survey captures existing cases at one time point, so it cannot give incidence.","p":60,"f":"Prevalence = 78 / 410 ≈ 19.0%"},{"q":"A newspaper reads falling depression prevalence with age in one survey as 'rising in each new generation'. What is the flaw?","a":"In a single survey, age and birth cohort are tied together, so an age effect and a generational effect look identical. Repeated surveys or a cohort study are needed to separate them.","p":60},{"q":"A cross-sectional survey links cannabis use with depression. Why can it not show cannabis causes depression?","a":"Exposure and outcome are measured at the same moment, so temporality is unknown: either could have come first.","p":58},{"q":"A national mental health survey draws a fresh random sample every seven years to track prevalence. What design is this, and why is it not a cohort?","a":"Repeated (serial) cross-sectional survey. Different people are sampled each round; a cohort follows the same people.","p":61},{"q":"National suicide figures are plotted for 20 years either side of a law limiting analgesic pack sizes. Name the design and a typical analysis.","a":"Ecological time-trend study: exposure and outcome are population figures over calendar time, often analysed as an interrupted time series.","p":62}]},{"t":"Case-control studies","s":63,"e":68,"c":[{"q":"300 psychosis cases and 600 controls: 90 cases and 60 controls used high-potency cannabis daily. Calculate the odds ratio.","a":"OR ≈ 3.86. Odds of exposure: cases 90/210 = 0.429, controls 60/540 = 0.111. Cases had about 3.9 times the odds of past daily use.","p":64,"f":"OR = ad / bc = (90 × 540) / (60 × 210) ≈ 3.86"},{"q":"A trainee divides 30% exposed cases by 10% exposed controls and calls it a relative risk of 3. Why is that invalid?","a":"The researcher fixed the numbers of cases and controls, so risk of the outcome cannot be calculated. Only the odds ratio is valid in a case-control study.","p":64},{"q":"When can a case-control odds ratio be read as a risk ratio?","a":"When the outcome is rare (under about 10%). With a true risk ratio of 2, the OR is 2.02 at 1% baseline risk but 3.50 at 30%.","p":66},{"q":"As an outcome becomes common, in which direction does the odds ratio diverge from the risk ratio?","a":"Further from 1. It exaggerates the effect, whether harmful or protective. Matching or adding controls does not change this.","p":66},{"q":"For which research questions is a case-control design the design of choice?","a":"Rare outcomes and diseases with long latency. Sampling on outcome makes it quick and cheap, and allows several exposures to be studied.","p":63},{"q":"In a matched case-control study, 40 pairs have only the case exposed and 10 pairs only the control exposed. Calculate the matched odds ratio.","a":"OR = 4. Concordant pairs carry no information; matched data need matched analysis (McNemar, conditional logistic regression).","p":68,"f":"Matched OR = b / c = 40 / 10 = 4"},{"q":"Within a primary care cohort, each new dementia case is matched to two people still registered and dementia-free on that date. Name the design and sampling method.","a":"Nested case-control study with incidence density (risk-set) sampling. Controls are drawn from those at risk at each case's date, and a control may later become a case.","p":66},{"q":"Why does a nested case-control study with risk-set sampling avoid recall bias and the rare-disease assumption?","a":"Exposure comes from records made before the outcome, so no one has to remember. Risk-set sampling makes the OR estimate the incidence rate ratio directly.","p":67},{"q":"An interviewer who knows which participants have bipolar disorder probes harder about childhood trauma. Name the bias and fix.","a":"Interviewer (observer) bias. Blind interviewers to case status and use a structured interview.","p":67},{"q":"Publicity means women on one contraceptive are screened for depression more often. A case-control study then links the pill with depression. What bias?","a":"Detection (surveillance) bias: exposure prompted closer looking, so the outcome was found more often among exposed women.","p":67}]},{"t":"Cohort studies","s":69,"e":74,"c":[{"q":"Of 1,200 people with hearing loss, 174 develop dementia in 10 years; of 4,800 without, 325 do. Calculate the relative risk.","a":"RR ≈ 2.14. Risk is 14.5% with hearing loss against 6.8% without, so about twice the risk.","p":70,"f":"RR = Ie / Iu = (174 / 1,200) / (325 / 4,800) ≈ 2.14"},{"q":"Using the same cohort (risks 14.5% and 6.8%), calculate the attributable risk and the number needed to harm.","a":"AR ≈ 7.7 per 100; NNH ≈ 13. About one extra dementia case for every 13 people with hearing loss over 10 years.","p":70,"f":"AR = Ie − Iu ≈ 0.077; NNH = 1 / AR ≈ 13"},{"q":"With RR 2.14, what proportion of dementia among people with hearing loss is attributable to hearing loss, assuming causation?","a":"About 53%. This is the attributable fraction in the exposed.","p":70,"f":"AR% = (RR − 1) / RR = 1.14 / 2.14 ≈ 53%"},{"q":"The whole cohort's 10-year risk is 8.3% and the unexposed risk 6.8%. Calculate the population attributable risk and fraction.","a":"PAR ≈ 1.5 per 100; PAF ≈ 19%. Assuming causation, about a fifth of the cohort's dementia would vanish without hearing loss.","p":72,"f":"PAR = It − Iu = AR × Pe; PAF = PAR / It ≈ 1.5 / 8.3"},{"q":"Using the hearing-loss cohort, why is the odds ratio (2.34) a misleading stand-in for the relative risk (2.14)?","a":"The OR is valid in a cohort, but because the outcome is not rare it overstates the RR. Cohorts can give risk directly, so report the RR.","p":70,"f":"OR = ad / bc = (174 × 4,475) / (1,026 × 325) ≈ 2.34"},{"q":"What key causal advantage does a cohort design have over case-control and cross-sectional studies?","a":"Temporality. Exposure is measured before the outcome occurs, so the order of events is clear, and incidence can be measured directly.","p":69},{"q":"Lithium prescribing from 1995 to 2000 is taken from records, then renal registry data are searched to 2023 for kidney failure. What design?","a":"Retrospective (historical) cohort. People are grouped by past exposure and followed forward in records; both had already happened when the study began. It still gives incidence and RR.","p":72},{"q":"A colleague says a retrospective cohort needs fewer participants than a prospective one. Correct them.","a":"Wrong: it needs the same sample size. It only saves waiting time. Its main threats are attrition, detection bias and confounding.","p":71},{"q":"Participants enter a psychosis cohort at staggered dates and are followed for varying lengths. How should incidence be expressed?","a":"As an incidence rate: new cases per person-years of follow-up. Compare groups with the incidence rate ratio.","p":73,"f":"Incidence rate = events / person-time"},{"q":"A cohort study of dementia recruits some people with mild undiagnosed dementia at baseline. What is wrong?","a":"The denominator must be people at risk. Including prevalent cases at baseline contaminates incidence, and could make exposure appear to follow the outcome.","p":71}]},{"t":"Self-controlled case series","s":75,"e":80,"c":[{"q":"What does a self-controlled case series compare, and who is sampled?","a":"Only cases; each person's risk-window rate against their own control-time rate. The output is a relative incidence (incidence rate ratio), so no separate control group is ever recruited.","p":75},{"q":"In an SCCS, 7 falls occur in 336 risk-window days and 8 falls in 4,044 control days. What is the crude relative incidence?","a":"About 10.5. Risk rate 7 ÷ 336 = 20.8 per 1,000 days; control rate 8 ÷ 4,044 = 2.0 per 1,000 days. Falls are roughly ten times more frequent after starting the drug.","p":76,"f":"RI = (events in risk ÷ risk time) ÷ (events in control ÷ control time)"},{"q":"A trainee notes 7 falls in risk windows versus 8 in control time and concludes the drug is protective. What is the flaw?","a":"Comparing counts, not rates. Risk windows are short, so seven events packed into 336 days is far denser than eight spread over 4,044 days. Always divide by person-time.","p":77},{"q":"Why does an SCCS automatically control for genes, sex, frailty and deprivation, but not for age or season?","a":"Fixed characteristics cancel because each person is compared with themselves. Age and season change across the observation year, so they are time-varying and must be adjusted for, typically in conditional Poisson regression.","p":76},{"q":"Hyponatraemia relative incidence is 3.22 in days 1–7, 4.39 in days 8–14 and 0.80 (0.52–1.24) in days 29–56 after starting an SSRI. Interpret.","a":"Risk peaks in the second week, then returns to baseline by days 29–56, where the interval includes 1. Splitting the risk period into windows shows when monitoring sodium matters most.","p":78},{"q":"Why might an SCCS separate a 14-day pre-exposure window from control time?","a":"The event or its prodrome may delay or prompt starting the drug. Setting that time aside stops it distorting the baseline rate, protecting the assumption that events do not affect later exposure.","p":79},{"q":"List three key assumptions of a standard self-controlled case series.","a":"Events are independent or rare; the event does not alter the chance of later exposure; and the event does not cut short observation. Death breaks the last one, so needs a modified method.","p":77},{"q":"Fatal overdose after starting an opioid substitute is the outcome of interest. Why is a standard SCCS unsuitable?","a":"Death ends the observation period, so the person cannot contribute later control time. Standard SCCS assumes the event does not curtail follow-up; a modified method would be required.","p":77},{"q":"Benzodiazepine use on the day of a crash is compared with use on the same weekday in the four previous weeks. Name the design.","a":"Case-crossover study. It compares exposure in a hazard window just before the event with earlier reference windows in the same person, whereas SCCS compares event rates across time windows.","p":79},{"q":"Children exposed to an antiepileptic in pregnancy are compared for ADHD with unexposed siblings. What does this design control for?","a":"A sibling comparison removes confounders brothers and sisters share, such as family environment and part of their genes. It cannot remove factors that differ between siblings.","p":80}]},{"t":"Choosing the study design","s":81,"e":86,"c":[{"q":"Match each question type to its design: prevalence, incidence or prognosis, treatment effect, patient experience.","a":"Prevalence: cross-sectional survey. Incidence and prognosis: cohort. Treatment effect: randomised trial, if ethical and feasible. Experience: qualitative study. Always name the question before choosing the design.","p":81},{"q":"Researchers want risk factors for neuroleptic malignant syndrome, looking at dose, titration speed, dehydration and lithium. Which design is most efficient?","a":"Case-control study. The outcome is rare, so start from the cases and compare them with antipsychotic users without the reaction. Several exposures can be examined at once, each giving an odds ratio.","p":82},{"q":"Why is a cohort a poor choice for a rare outcome, even though it is valid?","a":"It needs enormous numbers to accumulate enough cases. At a 0.5% baseline risk, detecting a doubling needs about 9,346 people in a cohort versus 344 in a case-control study.","p":82},{"q":"A team wants to study babies exposed to lithium in pregnancy and several childhood outcomes. Which design, and why?","a":"Cohort, for example from a pregnancy register. With a rare exposure few controls would be exposed, so a case-control study is inefficient; a cohort samples on exposure and can follow several outcomes.","p":84},{"q":"In the design flowchart, what are the first two questions that separate an RCT from an observational study?","a":"Did the researcher assign the exposure? If yes, was allocation random? Assigned and random means RCT; assigned but not random is a non-randomised trial; not assigned means observational.","p":82},{"q":"Among observational studies, how does the way people are chosen identify the design? Include group-level data.","a":"Chosen by exposure and followed up: cohort. Chosen by outcome and looked back: case-control. Both measured at one time: cross-sectional. If the unit is a group, not a person: ecological.","p":83},{"q":"Why should you not propose an RCT to test whether cannabis causes psychosis?","a":"Deliberately exposing people to a suspected harm is unethical. Use the best observational design, such as a cohort, and judge causation carefully against confounding and reverse causation.","p":83},{"q":"Twin correlations for conduct problems are 0.70 (MZ) and 0.50 (DZ). Estimate heritability and shared environment.","a":"h² = 0.40 and c² = 0.30. The MZ–DZ gap of 0.20 is doubled for heritability. Unique environment is 1 − 0.70 = 0.30. Forgetting to double gives the tempting wrong answer 0.20.","p":85,"f":"h² = 2(rMZ − rDZ); c² = 2rDZ − rMZ; e² = 1 − rMZ"},{"q":"A trait has heritability 0.8. A patient asks whether this means they have an 80% chance of inheriting it. How do you answer?","a":"No. Heritability is the share of variation across a population explained by genetic differences. It says nothing about an individual's risk and can differ between populations.","p":83},{"q":"Contrast what family, adoption, linkage and genome-wide association studies can each show.","a":"Family: clustering, but genes and homes are confounded. Adoption: separates genes from rearing, though the womb is shared. Linkage: rare strong variants in multiplex families. GWAS: common small-effect variants, using p < 5×10⁻⁸.","p":83}]},{"t":"RCTs: randomisation and blinding","s":87,"e":92,"c":[{"q":"Name the three protective steps of an RCT and the bias or problem each prevents.","a":"Sequence generation balances known and unknown confounders; allocation concealment prevents selection bias; blinding prevents performance and detection bias. Each step works at a different moment in the trial.","p":87},{"q":"A computer-generated list is pinned up in the research office; the nurse checks it before deciding whether to enrol. What has failed?","a":"Allocation concealment. The recruiter can foresee the next allocation and steer milder or sicker patients into an arm. Baseline imbalance in severity is the typical signature of this selection bias.","p":88},{"q":"Distinguish allocation concealment from blinding. Which is possible in every trial?","a":"Concealment hides the next allocation before entry; blinding hides the group after entry. Concealment is always achievable, even in surgery or psychotherapy, where blinding often is not.","p":88},{"q":"Give three adequate methods of allocation concealment.","a":"A central web or telephone service; pharmacy-coded identical packs; sequentially numbered, opaque, sealed envelopes. All stop the recruiter seeing the next allocation until the patient is irreversibly enrolled.","p":88},{"q":"A trial allocates by odd or even hospital number and calls itself randomised. What is wrong?","a":"It is quasi-randomisation. The allocation can be predicted, so it cannot be concealed. The same applies to date of birth, day of the week or simple alternation.","p":89},{"q":"A CONSORT diagram shows 612 assessed and 222 excluded; 18 of 195 drug and 11 of 195 placebo patients lack outcomes. Interpret.","a":"390 were randomised (64% of those assessed). Losses are 9.2% versus 5.6%: unequal attrition is a warning sign. The excluded box shows to whom the result may not apply.","p":89,"f":"Loss = missing ÷ number allocated"},{"q":"Why use permuted blocks, and why vary the block size in an open-label trial?","a":"Blocks keep arm sizes nearly equal throughout recruitment; with blocks of 4 the gap never exceeds 2. Randomly varying size, say 4 or 6, stops recruiters guessing the last allocation in a block.","p":92},{"q":"Does randomisation guarantee identical groups at baseline? Use the simulation of trials of 50 versus 500.","a":"No; it balances on average. About 49% of 50-patient trials had a 10-point imbalance in severity, against 2% of 500-patient trials. Small trials can be unbalanced by chance alone.","p":91},{"q":"A 70-patient, four-site trial needs balance on site, sex, smoking and baseline PANSS. Stratification would create too many strata. What allocation method suits?","a":"Minimisation. Each new patient goes to the arm that minimises overall imbalance across factors, usually with a random element. For one strong factor in a small trial, stratified block randomisation suffices.","p":91},{"q":"A report says a trial was 'double-blind'. What should a critical reader check?","a":"Exactly who was blinded: participants, clinicians, outcome assessors or analysts. The term is used inconsistently, so a good report names each group rather than relying on the label.","p":89}]},{"t":"Power, sample size and precision","s":93,"e":97,"c":[{"q":"What four ingredients are needed to calculate a trial's sample size?","a":"Alpha, power (1 − β), the smallest difference worth detecting, and the outcome's standard deviation. Fix them at the design stage; together they set how many people to recruit.","p":93},{"q":"A trial seeks a 3-point PHQ-9 difference, SD 6, two-sided alpha 0.05, 80% power. How many per arm?","a":"About 63 per arm. Effect size d = 3 ÷ 6 = 0.5. Then 2 × (1.96 + 0.84)² ÷ 0.25 = 62.8, rounded up. The exact t-test gives 64.","p":94,"f":"n per arm = 2(z₁₋α/₂ + z₁₋β)² σ² ÷ Δ²"},{"q":"The calculation gives 63 per arm, with 20% expected dropout. How many should be recruited per arm?","a":"79 per arm. Divide, do not multiply: 63 ÷ 0.8 = 78.8, rounded up. Multiplying by 1.2 gives 76 and leaves the trial short once a fifth drop out.","p":94,"f":"n to recruit = n ÷ (1 − dropout proportion)"},{"q":"A team halves the difference it wants to detect. What happens to the required sample size, and why?","a":"It quadruples. The difference is squared in the denominator, so halving it multiplies n by four. The SD is squared on top, so a noisier outcome is equally costly.","p":94,"f":"n ∝ σ² ÷ Δ²"},{"q":"Define power, alpha and beta in plain terms, with their usual values.","a":"Power is the chance of detecting a real effect, usually 80–90%. Alpha is the false-positive (type I) rate, usually 0.05. Beta is the false-negative (type II) rate, 0.1–0.2. Power = 1 − β.","p":97,"f":"Power = 1 − β"},{"q":"On a diagram of two sampling distributions, where are power, beta and alpha?","a":"Power is the area under the true-difference curve beyond the critical value. Beta is the rest of that curve. Alpha is both tails under the null curve beyond the critical values.","p":95},{"q":"A trial of 40 per arm finds no significant effect for an expected d of 0.3. Can you conclude the treatment does not work?","a":"No. Its power is only about 26%, so it would usually miss a real small effect. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence; read the confidence interval instead.","p":94},{"q":"After a negative trial, the authors report a low post hoc power. Why is this unhelpful?","a":"Post hoc power is just a restatement of the p value and adds no information. The confidence interval shows which effect sizes remain compatible with the data.","p":95},{"q":"Does choosing a stricter alpha of 0.01 increase power? How much does moving from 80% to 90% power cost?","a":"No; a stricter alpha lowers power for the same sample. Going from 80% to 90% power needs about a third more participants: for d = 0.5, 64 rises to 86 per arm.","p":95},{"q":"What is precision, and how much does doubling the sample size improve it?","a":"Precision is the confidence interval's width, which shrinks with 1/√n. Doubling n narrows it by only about 30%; quadrupling n halves it. Judge trials by interval width, not by point estimate size.","p":96,"f":"CI width ∝ 1 ÷ √n"}]},{"t":"Intention-to-treat and missing data","s":98,"e":103,"c":[{"q":"What does intention-to-treat analysis mean, and what question does it answer?","a":"Everyone randomised is analysed in the arm they were allocated, whatever they took. It preserves randomisation's balance and estimates the effect of offering the treatment: effectiveness.","p":98},{"q":"Drug arm: 75 of 150 adherent and 10 of 50 stoppers respond. Placebo arm: 51 of 170 and 15 of 30 switchers. ITT difference?","a":"9.5 percentage points. Drug: 85 ÷ 200 = 42.5%. Placebo: 66 ÷ 200 = 33.0%. NNT is about 11. Stoppers and switchers stay in their randomised arms.","p":99,"f":"ITT difference = responders A ÷ randomised A − responders B ÷ randomised B"},{"q":"In that trial, per-protocol gives a 20.0-point difference and as-treated 22.3. Why prefer the smaller ITT result?","a":"Both alternatives break randomisation. Those who stopped the drug did badly, so dropping or regrouping them flatters the drug. A bigger difference is not a better analysis; only ITT compares like with like.","p":100},{"q":"Define per-protocol and as-treated analyses and say what each loses.","a":"Per-protocol keeps only adherent patients, approximating efficacy but biased because adherers differ. As-treated regroups by treatment actually received, losing randomisation entirely.","p":99},{"q":"A trial excludes patients who never took a dose but calls its analysis ITT. What is the correct label?","a":"Modified ITT at best. It analyses people as randomised but drops a pre-defined subgroup. True ITT includes everyone randomised.","p":100},{"q":"Why should a non-inferiority trial report both ITT and per-protocol analyses?","a":"ITT is usually conservative in superiority trials, but in non-inferiority trials non-adherence pulls arms together and can flatter similarity. Both analyses should agree before non-inferiority is claimed.","p":99},{"q":"Define MCAR, MAR and MNAR, and say which methods assume MAR.","a":"MCAR: missingness unrelated to anything. MAR: explained by observed data. MNAR: depends on the unseen values themselves. Multiple imputation and mixed models (MMRM) are valid under MAR.","p":103},{"q":"A patient drops out at week 4 with MADRS 27; the week-12 value is set at 27. Name the method and its flaw.","a":"Last observation carried forward. It assumes no change after dropout, which can bias in either direction and understates uncertainty. Calling it conservative is a trap.","p":101},{"q":"How do baseline observation carried forward and multiple imputation differ in filling a missing week-12 score?","a":"BOCF returns the patient to their baseline score. Multiple imputation draws several plausible values from a model fitted to observed data, then combines results so uncertainty is reflected.","p":101},{"q":"Complete cases give +15.0 points (significant); the worst case for drug gives −4.0 (−15.3 to +7.3). What do you conclude?","a":"The result is fragile: it depends on what happened to the missing patients. A tipping-point analysis asks how extreme the assumption about missing outcomes must be to overturn the conclusion.","p":102}]},{"t":"Crossover, cluster and factorial trials","s":104,"e":109,"c":[{"q":"Why does a crossover trial need fewer participants, and which conditions suit it?","a":"Each person receives both treatments, so between-person variation drops out. It suits chronic, stable conditions with treatments of quick onset and offset, not cure, death or a changing illness.","p":105},{"q":"In an AB/BA crossover with no washout, the drug-first group remains 6 points improved during the first placebo week. Name the problem and remedy.","a":"Carry-over, which biases towards no difference. Remedy: a washout long enough for the drug's effect to fade, or analyse period 1 only.","p":105},{"q":"How does a period effect differ from carry-over in a crossover trial?","a":"A period effect shifts outcomes in period 2 for both sequences, whatever the treatment. Carry-over affects only the group coming off the active treatment, because its effect lingers.","p":106},{"q":"Why is a crossover unsuitable for a trial of a therapy teaching coping skills?","a":"The outcome does not reverse: once a skill is learned it persists into the next period. Crossovers also fail for cure, death or relapse needing admission.","p":106},{"q":"A cluster trial has 780 patients in practices of 20, ICC 0.05. What is the design effect and effective sample size?","a":"Design effect 1.95; effective sample about 400. Ignoring clustering would give falsely narrow confidence intervals.","p":109,"f":"Design effect = 1 + (m − 1) × ICC; effective n = N ÷ design effect"},{"q":"Describe a stepped-wedge cluster trial and its main weakness.","a":"Every cluster starts on usual care and crosses to the intervention at a randomised time until all have it. Later periods contain more intervention, so secular trends confound the effect; adjust for period.","p":106},{"q":"Non-inferiority margin is 10 points. A trial shows +2.0 (−3.9 to +7.9). What do you conclude?","a":"Non-inferior but not superior. The whole interval lies above −10, excluding a clinically important loss, but it crosses 0, so superiority is not shown.","p":107,"f":"Non-inferior if lower CI limit > −Δ"},{"q":"Margin 10 points. A trial shows −5.0 (−8.5 to −1.5). Interpret it.","a":"Non-inferior, yet statistically worse than standard. The interval lies between −Δ and 0, so the loss is smaller than the margin. Readers must judge whether that loss is acceptable.","p":108},{"q":"A superiority trial finds no significant difference, and the authors claim the treatments are equivalent. What is wrong?","a":"Non-significance is not equivalence. Showing equivalence needs a pre-specified margin and a whole confidence interval inside ±Δ. Margins must be set on clinical grounds before seeing data.","p":106,"f":"Equivalent if whole CI within −Δ to +Δ"},{"q":"On a PRECIS-2 wheel, what do scores near the rim mean, and what does the wheel not show?","a":"Near the rim (5) is pragmatic, asking 'does it work in practice?'; near the centre (1) is explanatory, 'can it work?'. PRECIS-2 describes design choices, not risk of bias.","p":108}]},{"t":"Trial phases and validity","s":110,"e":114,"c":[{"q":"A new antipsychotic, already shown to be tolerable, is now randomised at three doses against placebo in 240 people with acute schizophrenia for 6 weeks. Which phase is this?","a":"Phase II (IIb, dose-finding). It is the first look in patients at whether the drug works and at what dose: moderate size, several doses, short efficacy signal. Phase I tolerability has already been done.","p":110},{"q":"What question does a Phase 0 study answer, and roughly how many people take part?","a":"Does the drug reach its target? About 10 to 15 people get tiny, non-therapeutic microdoses, often with PET imaging, to check pharmacokinetics or target binding. It says nothing about efficacy.","p":111},{"q":"A trainee says Phase I trials always use healthy volunteers. Why is this wrong in psychiatry?","a":"Antipsychotic and some other psychotropic Phase I studies often recruit stable patients. Their side effects are hard to justify exposing healthy people to, so rising-dose safety work is done in those with the illness.","p":111},{"q":"A drug's two Phase III trials (1,350 patients in total) showed no serious hepatotoxicity. Can the licence holder claim the drug causes no rare liver harm?","a":"No. Phase III trials are too small and too short to detect harms rarer than about 1 in 1,000. Rare and long-term harms surface in Phase IV, through large cohorts and spontaneous reporting such as the UK Yellow Card scheme.","p":111},{"q":"Of 1,000 adults with depression on a community caseload, 270 meet an antidepressant trial's criteria. What percentage is eligible, and what does this threaten?","a":"27%; 73% are excluded. The excluded (suicidal, substance-using, comorbid) are often those clinicians most need evidence for, so external validity suffers even if internal validity is excellent.","p":112,"f":"Eligible % = 270 / 1,000 × 100 = 27%"},{"q":"Define internal validity and external validity in one line each.","a":"Internal: is the result true, meaning free of bias, so the effect is due to treatment? External: is it true for my patients, meaning does it generalise beyond the trial? A trial can excel on the first and fail the second.","p":110},{"q":"A pilot of a self-harm group intervention recruits 1.6 per site per month (go ≥ 2.0, stop < 1.0), retains 84% (go ≥ 80%) and achieves 45% attendance (stop < 50%). What decision follows?","a":"Amend recruitment, and rethink delivery. Retention passes, recruitment is in the amend zone (add sites or referral routes), and attendance falls in the stop zone, so the intervention as delivered must change and probably be piloted again.","p":113},{"q":"A team plans to power its main trial on the effect size seen in its 58-person pilot. What is the flaw?","a":"A pilot tests feasibility, not efficacy. Its effect estimate is so imprecise (very wide confidence intervals) that powering on it risks a badly sized main trial. Judge the pilot against pre-set progression criteria instead.","p":110},{"q":"An uncontrolled study shows patients improve after starting a new drug. Name four things other than the drug that could explain the improvement.","a":"Regression to the mean, natural recovery, history (outside events) and testing effects. Only a randomised control group, which experiences all of these too, separates them from a true drug effect.","p":110},{"q":"After a 6-week placebo-controlled trial, everyone is offered the active drug for 52 weeks. What is this design called, and can it show efficacy?","a":"An open-label extension. It gathers longer-term safety data and gives placebo patients access to the drug, but without randomisation or blinding it cannot demonstrate efficacy.","p":114}]},{"t":"Qualitative approaches","s":115,"e":120,"c":[{"q":"Is qualitative research typically inductive or deductive, and what does that mean for how it handles hypotheses?","a":"Inductive. It works bottom up, building understanding, theory and new hypotheses from words, actions and meanings, rather than testing a hypothesis fixed in advance.","p":115},{"q":"Researchers code each interview, compare new codes with earlier ones, choose the next participant to test an emerging idea, and stop when nothing new appears. Which approach is this?","a":"Grounded theory. Its fingerprints are constant comparison, theoretical sampling and theoretical saturation, with collection and analysis running together, producing an explanatory model of a process.","p":116},{"q":"What is the end product of a grounded theory study, and how does that differ from a list of themes?","a":"A theory or model of a process, organised around one core category. A list of themes describes; a grounded theory explains how the parts connect. Glaser and Strauss described the method in 1967.","p":116},{"q":"Put the three grounded theory coding stages in order and say what each does.","a":"Open coding labels data line by line; axial coding links codes into categories; selective coding chooses and refines the core category that ties everything together. Memos record the analyst's emerging ideas throughout.","p":118},{"q":"A study interviews people about their experiences; a reviewer calls it grounded theory. What must you look for before agreeing?","a":"Constant comparison, theoretical sampling and a theory as output. Interviews alone do not make grounded theory; without these markers it is more likely thematic or descriptive work.","p":117},{"q":"How does theoretical sampling differ from purposive sampling?","a":"Purposive sampling chooses people because they have the experience of interest. Theoretical sampling chooses the next person because the emerging analysis needs a particular idea or category tested.","p":117},{"q":"A researcher spends months on an inpatient ward as a participant observer to understand its culture. Which approach is this?","a":"Ethnography: understanding a culture from the inside through immersion and participant observation, rather than through interviews about it.","p":117},{"q":"A study of derealisation has the researcher set aside her clinical assumptions before analysis. Which approach, and how does it differ from IPA?","a":"Descriptive (Husserlian) phenomenology; setting assumptions aside is bracketing (epoché). IPA instead accepts that the researcher's interpretation is part of the analysis, exploring what an experience means to a small, similar group.","p":119},{"q":"Distinguish conversation analysis from discourse analysis.","a":"Conversation analysis studies how talk is organised: turn-taking, pauses, overlaps. Discourse analysis studies what language does: how it constructs meanings such as 'risk', and power.","p":117},{"q":"A trust studies its single new crisis house over a year, using interviews, meeting minutes, bed-use data and observation, to explain how the rollout went. Which approach is this?","a":"A case study: one bounded case (service, event or person) studied in context from several sources. It aims to explain how and why, not to generalise statistically.","p":119}]},{"t":"Qualitative sampling and saturation","s":121,"e":129,"c":[{"q":"A reviewer criticises a qualitative interview study for having no power calculation. Is this valid?","a":"No. Qualitative studies do not estimate a percentage, so they need neither a random sample nor a power calculation. Sample size is set by saturation (or 'information power').","p":121},{"q":"New themes per interview were 7, 5, 4, 3, 3, 2, 1, 2, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0. With a rule of stopping after 3 consecutive empty interviews, when was saturation reached?","a":"Interview 11, the last to add a theme; interviews 12 to 14 confirm it. Interview 10 is a trap: one empty interview breaks no rule of three, and interview 11 then adds one.","p":122,"f":"Total themes = 7 + 5 + 4 + 3 + 3 + 2 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 0 + 1 = 29"},{"q":"Why can saturation not be judged if all transcripts are coded at the end of data collection?","a":"Saturation means new data add nothing new, which you only know if analysis runs alongside collection. A stopping rule set in advance makes the judgement transparent rather than decided by eye afterwards.","p":122},{"q":"Distinguish data saturation from theoretical saturation.","a":"Data saturation: further data add no new themes. Theoretical saturation, used in grounded theory: further data add no new properties to the categories, so the categories stop developing.","p":122},{"q":"Participants are deliberately spread across four age bands and four care pathways, with no random draw and no fixed quota. Which sampling strategy is this?","a":"Purposive maximum variation sampling. Seeking the widest range of relevant experience lets shared themes be told apart from those tied to one group. Stratified random sampling would draw randomly within strata.","p":124},{"q":"How does snowball sampling differ from theoretical sampling, and when is snowball useful?","a":"In snowball sampling participants recruit others they know, useful for hidden groups with no list. In theoretical sampling the emerging theory decides who is approached next.","p":123},{"q":"A study of families includes only those whose relative was first detained in the last 12 months. Which sampling strategy is this?","a":"Criterion sampling: everyone who meets a predefined criterion is selected. It is purposive, chosen for relevance rather than to estimate prevalence, so calling it 'biased' misses the point.","p":125},{"q":"An interviewer opens with 'Tell me about the time since your diagnosis' and follows wherever the person leads. What type of interview is this, and which type is commonest?","a":"Unstructured (in-depth, narrative): the participant controls the agenda. Semi-structured interviews, using a topic guide with flexible order and probes, are commonest because they balance depth with focus and comparability.","p":125},{"q":"A team plans one focus group of 22 carers. What is wrong, and what should they do?","a":"Too large. Run several groups of about 6 to 10 people, with a facilitator and an observer. In focus groups the interaction between members is itself the data, which a huge group stifles.","p":123},{"q":"Name the four criteria for rigour in qualitative research, and two techniques that build it.","a":"Credibility, transferability, dependability and confirmability. Built through triangulation (comparing sources, methods, researchers or theories), reflexivity, respondent validation, negative case analysis and an audit trail.","p":127}]}];
